<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Kadesh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1274-bce">1274 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a> · Also known as Kadesh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Kadesh was fought in 1274 BCE near Kadesh on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> between the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> and the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a>. It is one of the best-documented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> battles of antiquity and ended without a decisive result, despite Egyptian claims of victory. It was followed later by a peace treaty between the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh.jpg" alt="Battle of Kadesh" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt_Abou_Simbel6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After expelling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> around 1550 BCE, the rulers of the Egyptian New Kingdom reclaimed their borders and fought as far north as the Orontes, but Egyptian influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> declined in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> show. In the Nineteenth Dynasty Seti I set out to restore the empire. He reoccupied abandoned posts, made an informal peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and captured Kadesh and Amurru, with his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> campaigning beside him. In his fourth year Ramesses marched into Syria, and in the spring of his fifth year, in May 1274 BCE, he set out from his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a>. The Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> gave the recovery of Amurru as his reason for marching south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Ramesses led four divisions, named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, Re, Set and Ptah, together with Sherden troops and a contingent called the Ne'arin. Muwatalli had gathered allies, and Ramesses recorded nineteen of them. Two captured nomads, who were Hittite spies, told Ramesses that the Hittite army was far off at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and he hurried towards Kadesh with the Amun division while the others were strung out behind him. Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> then crossed the river and struck the Re division in the open, scattering it, and went on to attack the Egyptian camp.</p><p>Ramesses, by his own account abandoned and surrounded, rallied his bodyguard and the survivors and led several charges. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> stopped to plunder the camp and were driven back towards the Orontes. Muwatalli sent another thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, but the Ne'arin arrived suddenly and the Ptah division threatened the Hittite rear. After six unsuccessful charges the Hittites were pinned against the river, and many drowned trying to swim it. They kept Kadesh itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Unable to besiege the walled city, Ramesses went south and then back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, proclaiming victory. Hittite records tell a different story, and modern historians regard the battle as a draw. Muwatalli went on to capture the Egyptian province of Upi. Ramesses took Dapur and Tunip in his eighth and ninth years, but they returned to Hittite control within a year. A peace treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> was signed in the twenty-first year of Ramesses's reign, about 1258 BCE. It was inscribed on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> tablet; a clay copy was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> and an Egyptian version survives on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a>.</p><p>More texts and wall reliefs record this battle than any other in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Ancient Near East</a>, almost all from the Egyptian side. The main sources are the Poem and the Bulletin, repeated in temples at Abydos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesseum">Ramesseum</a>, and the bombastic nature of Ramesses's version is recognised.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Kadesh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ramesses II and the Hittites made peace in 1258 BCE in the earliest treaty to survive in both parties' versions, settling a stalemate after the <b>Battle of Kadesh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Battle of Kadesh</b> in 1274 BCE, fought against Ramesses II on the Orontes River under Muwatalli II, is often described as the largest chariot engagement of antiquity, with perhaps five thousand chariots involved on both sides.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>As a Hittite vassal Ugarit had to send troops to its overlord’s wars, and Egyptian accounts list it among the allies of the Hittite king Muwatalli II at the <b>Battle of Kadesh</b> in 1274 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The New Kingdom added love poems, the Tale of the Two Brothers, the Great Hymn to the Aten and the poem on the battle of <b>Kadesh</b> of Ramesses II.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>…BCE the light two-wheeled chariot spread across the Near East, Egypt, and China, and the great powers of the age fought with chariots in their thousands; the <b>Battle of Kadesh</b>, fought between Egypt and the Hittites around 1274 BCE, may have been the largest chariot battle ever fought.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>The army fought in Nubia and the Levant: Ramesses II marched to the <b>Battle of Kadesh</b> in 1274 BCE with four divisions named after the gods Amun, Ra, Ptah and Seth.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
